ation or\n  deceptive-turn scenarios, and instrumental incentives alongside formal\n  self-verification limits.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request. For each selected check, put any term that must be\nstated literally in `must_mention`; use an empty list when no exact term is\nneeded. A search phrase is not an obligation: if HIPAA, a named theorem, a\nregulator, or another standard matters, name it in both the requirement and\n`must_mention`. Include at most two penalty-avoidance checks, such as a specific\nunsupported generalization the answer must avoid.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"term|evidence|mechanism|comparison|scope|safety|format|penalty\",\"requirement\":\"one atomic operational requirement\",\"must_mention\":[\"exact term\"],\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"one compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most eight additions, ranked by expected evaluation weight per answer\nword. Keep each requirement under 30 words, each reason under 35 words, at most\nthree exact terms, and at most one search lead. Do not combine distinct checks\nwith a list joined by \"and\". Use verdict \"pass\" only when no material implicit\ncheck exists. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nwrite a series of blog posts on important combinatorics topics in math olympiads. Include intuitive and detailed explanations, as well as example problems from top olympiads such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, IMO shortlist, etc. Provide correct solutions for those problems and the insight or takeaways from those solutions.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"scope\",\"requirement\":\"Topic map must include double counting, extremal principle, probabilistic method, plus a graph-modeling branch.\",\"must_mention\":[\"double counting\",\"extremal principle\",\"probabilistic method\"],\"reason\":\"A serious olympiad-combinatorics series should span counting, structural, existence, and modeling techniques rather than present disconnected elementary tricks.\",\"search_leads\":[\"standard olympiad combinatorics syllabus topics\"]},{\"kind\":\"format\",\"requirement\":\"State an explicit post sequence whose later installments reuse techniques established in earlier installments.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"A series needs pedagogical progression and prerequisites, not merely an unordered topic inventory.\",\"search_leads\":[\"combinatorics olympiad course progression syllabus\"]},{\"kind\":\"format\",\"requirement\":\"Apply the intuition→formal detail→sourced problem→complete solution→takeaway sequence in every post.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"The request repeats these deliverables implicitly for the series; providing them only selectively would be incomplete.\",\"search_leads\":[\"mathematics expository blog worked-example structure\"]},{\"kind\":\"evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Include national and international examples, each labeled with exact contest, year, and problem number or shortlist code.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Precise metadata permits verification, prevents invented attributions, and demonstrates the requested breadth of top-olympiad sources.\",\"search_leads\":[\"official olympiad combinatorics problem archive year number\"]},{\"kind\":\"safety\",\"requirement\":\"For every worked problem, justify each nontrivial implication and check equality cases, extremal cases, or construction validity where applicable.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Olympiad solutions often fail through hidden assumptions or unverified constructions; polished sketches are not necessarily correct solutions.\",\"search_leads\":[\"olympiad solution common gaps equality cases constructions\"]},{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"Solve at least one featured problem by two genuinely different methods, then compare their recognition cues and tradeoffs.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"An alternative viewpoint teaches method selection and reveals connections that a single official-style solution cannot.\",\"search_leads\":[\"olympiad combinatorics problem multiple solutions\"]},{\"kind\":\"safety\",\"requirement\":\"For each major technique, identify one common misuse or boundary condition that makes the method fail.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Novices need applicability limits to distinguish genuine technique recognition from superficial pattern matching.\",\"search_leads\":[\"common mistakes olympiad combinatorics methods\"]},{\"kind\":\"mechanism\",\"requirement\":\"Demonstrate one explicit cross-domain transfer by converting a non-graph formulation into graph theory language.\",\"must_mention\":[\"graph theory\"],\"reason\":\"A concrete reformulation teaches transferable modeling, a central skill in advanced olympiad combinatorics.\",\"search_leads\":[\"olympiad combinatorics graph reformulation example\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"kind": "scope", "requirement": "Topic map must include double counting, extremal principle, probabilistic method, plus a graph-modeling branch.", "must_mention": ["double counting", "extremal principle", "probabilistic method"], "reason": "A serious olympiad-combinatorics series should span counting, structural, existence, and modeling techniques rather than present disconnected elementary tricks.", "search_leads": ["standard olympiad combinatorics syllabus topics"]}, {"kind": "format",