, identify one common misuse or boundary condition that makes the method fail.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "Novices need applicability limits to distinguish genuine technique recognition from superficial pattern matching.", "search_leads": ["common mistakes olympiad combinatorics methods"]}, {"kind": "mechanism", "requirement": "Demonstrate one explicit cross-domain transfer by converting a non-graph formulation into graph theory language.", "must_mention": ["graph theory"], "reason": "A concrete reformulation teaches transferable modeling, a central skill in advanced olympiad combinatorics.", "search_leads": ["olympiad combinatorics graph reformulation example"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1131, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 3800}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "query": "Conduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.", "system": "You are the blind evaluator-expectation scout for a research system. You receive\nthe original request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output.\nPredict the small set of atomic, binary checks that a demanding knowledgeable\nreader would use to distinguish a complete answer from a merely plausible one.\nDo not answer the request, state facts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. Do not write broad wishlist items: each\naddition must express one independently checkable obligation. Check for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one; when the request joins\n  two central variables or populations, require at least one prior measurement\n  that studies that combination, not two unrelated adjacent studies;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- the exact proper name, acronym, governing law or standard, canonical method,\n  dataset, comparison baseline, or metric that generic prose would otherwise\n  paraphrase away; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: rank atomic checks by expected\n  evaluation value per answer word and prefer coverage over optional depth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument. For a recursively self-improving system, explicitly recall\n  escalation or intelligence-feedback dynamics, concealed-cooperation or\n  deceptive-turn scenarios, and instrumental incentives alongside formal\n  self-verification limits.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request. For each selected check, put any term that must be\nstated literally in `must_mention`; use an empty list when no exact term is\nneeded. A search phrase is not an obligation: if HIPAA, a named theorem, a\nregulator, or another standard matters, name it in both the requirement and\n`must_mention`. Include at most two penalty-avoidance checks, such as a specific\nunsupported generalization the answer must avoid.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"term|evidence|mechanism|comparison|scope|safety|format|penalty\",\"requirement\":\"one atomic operational requirement\",\"must_mention\":[\"exact term\"],\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"one compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most eight additions, ranked by expected evaluation weight per answer\nword. Keep each requirement under 30 words, each reason under 35 words, at most\nthree exact terms, and at most one search lead. Do not combine distinct checks\nwith a list joined by \"and\". Use verdict \"pass\" only when no material implicit\ncheck exists. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"scope\",\"requirement\":\"Define social media, set a justified time horizon, and state whether the judgment is global or geographically bounded.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"The conclusion is otherwise unfalsifiably broad because platforms, populations, and effects vary substantially across eras and regions.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media societal impact longitudinal global review\"]},{\"kind\":\"format\",\"requirement\":\"Evaluate connection, mental health, information quality, politics, economic activity, and culture as distinct domains with positive and negative evidence in each.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"A domain-based verdict is incomplete if major societal functions are omitted or benefits and harms are assessed asymmetrically.\",\"search_leads\":[\"systematic review social media societal impacts domains\"]},{\"kind\":\"evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Triangulate every domain with academic research, journalistic reporting, and a concrete case study rather than clustering evidence types in separate sections.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"The request explicitly requires all three evidence types, and domain-level triangulation prevents anecdotes or studies from carrying unrelated claims.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media impact academic journalism case studies\"]},{\"kind\":\"mechanism\",\"requirement\":\"Explain how engagement-based ranking, virality, microtargeting, and content moderation generate or amplify observed outcomes.\",\"must_mention\":[\"engagement-based ranking\",\"microtargeting\",\"content moderation\"],\"reason\":\"Mechanisms distinguish social media-specific effects from generic internet use and explain why benefits and harms can coexist.\",\"search_leads\":[\"engagement ranking virality microtargeting social media effects\"]},{\"kind\":\"evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Include at least one causal or quasi-experimental social-media study, explicitly separating causal estimates from correlational associations.\",\"must_mention\":[\"causal inference\"],\"reason\":\"Much social-media evidence is observational; evidentiary strength materially affects any definitive net-impact judgment.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media deactivation randomized experiment causal effects\"]},{\"kind\":\"scope\",\"requirement\":\"Include a non-Western case directly linking platform use to a societal outcome, such as Facebook’s role in Myanmar.\",\"must_mention\":[\"Myanmar\"],\"reason\":\"A society-wide conclusion based only on Western democracies would miss different institutions, conflict settings, and platform practices.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Facebook Myanmar Rohingya case study social media\"]},{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"Compare outcomes against plausible alternatives such as legacy media or pre-platform communication, not an idealized harm-free baseline.\",\"must_mention\":[\"counterfactual\"],\"reason\":\"Whether social media is beneficial depends on what would replace it; absolute catalogs of effects cannot establish net impact.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media versus traditional media counterfactual societal impact\"]},{\"kind\":\"mechanism\",\"requirement\":\"Derive the final verdict using explicit criteria for effect magnitude, population reach, duration, reversibility, distribution, and evidentiary confidence.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"A definitive judgment requires a transparent weighting rule rather than counting positive and negative examples equally.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social impact assessment weighting magnitude reach duration reversibility\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"kind": "scope", "requirement": "Define social media, set a justified time horizon, and state whether the judgment is global or geographically bounded.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "The conclusion is otherwise unfalsifiably broad because platforms, populations, and effects vary substantially across eras and regions.", "search_leads": ["social media societal impact longitudinal global review"]}, {"kind": "format", "requirement": "Evaluate connection, mental health, information quality, politics, economic activity, and culture as distinct domains with positive and negative evidence in each.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "A domain-based verdict is incomplete if major societal functions are omitted or benefits and harms are assessed asymmetrically.", "search_leads": ["systematic review social media societal impacts domains"]}, {"kind": "evidence", "requirement": "Triangulate every domain with academic research, journalistic reporting, and a concrete case study rather than clustering evidence types in separate sections.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "The request explicitly requires all three evidence types, and domain-level triangulation prevents anecdotes or studies from carrying unrelated claims.", "search_leads": ["social media impact academic journalism case studies"]}, {"kind": "mechanism", "requirement": "Explain how engagement-based ranking, virality, microtargeting, and content moderation generate or amplify observed outcomes.", "must_mention": ["engagement-based ranking", "microtargeting", "content moderation"], "reason": "Mechanisms distinguish social media-specific effects from generic internet use and explain why benefits and harms can coexist.", "search_leads": ["engagement ranking virality microtargeting social media effects"]}, {"kind": "evidence", "requirement": "Include at least one causal or quasi-experimental social-media study, explicitly separating causal estimates from correlational associations.", "must_mention": ["causal inference"], "reason": "Much social-media evidence is observational; evidentiary strength materially affects any definitive net-impact judgment.", "search_leads": ["social media deactivation randomized experiment causal effects"]}, {"kind": "scope", "requirement": "Include a non-Western case directly linking platform use to a societal outcome, such as Facebook’s role in Myanmar.", "must_mention": ["Myanmar"], "reason": "A society-wide conclusion based only on Western democracies would miss different institutions, conflict settings, and platform practices.", "search_leads": ["Facebook Myanmar Rohingya case study social media"]}, {"kind": "comparison", "requirement": "Compare outcomes against plausible alternatives such as legacy media or pre-platform communication, not an idealized harm-free baseline.", "must_mention": ["counterfactual"], "reason": "Whether social media is beneficial depends on what would replace it; absolute catalogs of effects cannot establish net impact.", "search_leads": ["social media versus traditional media counterfactual societal impact"]}, {"kind": "mechanism", "requirement": "Derive the final verdict using explicit criteria for effect magnitude, population reach, duration, reversibility, distribution, and evidentiary confidence.", "must_mention": [], "reason": "A definitive judgment requires a transparent weighting rule rather than counting positive and negative examples equally.", "search_leads": ["social impact assessment weighting magnitude reach duration reversibility"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1137, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 1464}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605391", "query": "Write a strategic business report advising a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup on how to enter and scale in the Southeast Asian market (focusing on countries like Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia). Your report should address: 1. A comparative analysis of consumer demand trends for alternative proteins in each target country. 2. Competitive landscape: key regional players, pricing, retail/distribution channels, and regulatory hurdles. 3. Cultural and dietary considerations that may influence adoption and positioning. 4. Go-to-market recommendations such as direct-to-consumer, B2B partnerships, and quick service restaurants. 5. Risks and opportunities associated with regulatory shifts, supply chain localization, and sustainability branding. Use regional market data, public reports, consumer surveys, and industry benchmarks. Assume your audience is a board of venture capital-backed startup executives seeking a data-driven, execution-ready plan.", "system": "You are the blind evaluator-expectation scout for a research system. You receive\nthe original request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output.\nPredict the small set of atomic, binary checks that a demanding knowledgeable\nreader would use to distinguish a complete answer from a merely plausible one.\nDo not answer the request, state facts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. Do not write broad wishlist items: each\naddition must express one independently checkable obligation. Check for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one; when the request joins\n  two central variables or populations, require at least one prior measurement\n  that studies that combination, not two unrelated adjacent studies;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- the exact proper name, acronym, governing law or standard, canonical method,\n  dataset, comparison baseline, or metric that generic prose would otherwise\n  paraphrase away; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: rank atomic checks by expected\n  evaluation value per answer word and prefer coverage over optional depth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument. For a recursively self-improving system, explicitly recall\n  escalation or intelligence-feedback dynamics, concealed-cooperation or\n  deceptive-turn scenarios, and instrumental incentives alongside formal\n  self-verification limits.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request. For each selected check, put any term that must be\nstated literally in `must_mention`; use an empty list when no exact term is\nneeded. A search phrase is not an obligation: if HIPAA, a named theorem, a\nregulator, or another standard matters, name it in both the requirement and\n`must_mention`. Include at most two penalty-avoidance checks, such as a specific\nunsupported generalization the answer must avoid.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"term|evidence|mechanism|comparison|scope|safety|format|penalty\",\"requirement\":\"one atomic operational requirement\",\"must_mention\":[\"exact term\"],\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"one compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most eight additions, ranked by expected evaluation weight per answer\nword. Keep each requirement under 30 words, each reason under 35 words, at most\nthree exact terms, and at most one search lead. Do not combine distinct checks\nwith a list joined by \"and\". Use verdict \"pass\" only when no material implicit\ncheck exists. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nWrite a strategic business report advising a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup on how to enter and scale in the Southeast Asian market (focusing on countries like Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia). Your report should address: 1. A comparative analysis of consumer demand trends for alternative proteins in each target country. 2. Competitive landscape: key regional players, pricing, retail/distribution channels, and regulatory hurdles. 3. Cultural and dietary considerations that may influence adoption and positioning. 4. Go-to-market recommendations such as direct-to-consumer, B2B partnerships, and quick service restaurants. 5. Risks and opportunities associated with regulatory shifts, supply chain localization, and sustainability branding. Use regional market data, public reports, consumer surveys, and industry benchmarks. Assume your audience is a board of venture capital-backed startup executives seeking a data-driven, execution-ready plan.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"kind\":\"evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Triangulate dated country-specific category size, growth, trial, repeat purchase, and willingness-to-pay evidence using at least one directly relevant source per country.\",\"must_mention\":[\"willingness to pay\"],\"reason\":\"Regional aggregates or adjacent food studies cannot support distinct demand forecasts for Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia plant-based meat consumer survey willingness to pay market size\"]},{\"kind\":\"term\",\"requirement\":\"Map product classification, approval timelines, labeling requirements, and halal applicability under each target country’s competent authority.\",\"must_mention\":[\"Singapore Food Agency (SFA)\",\"Thai Food and Drug Administration (Thai FDA)\",\"BPJPH\"],\"reason\":\"Regulatory feasibility, launch timing, packaging, and certification differ materially by jurisdiction and product formulation.\",\"search_leads\":[\"SFA novel food Thai FDA novel food BPJPH plant based meat requirements\"]},{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"Build country-channel unit economics covering landed cost, distributor margin, contribution margin, CAC payback, break-even volume, and downside sensitivities.\",\"must_mention\":[\"contribution margin\",\"CAC payback\",\"break-even volume\"],\"reason\":\"A board cannot approve entry based on market attractiveness without investable economics or explicit capital gates.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Southeast Asia food startup distributor margin retail listing fee ecommerce CAC benchmark\"]},{\"kind\":\"comparison\",\"requirement\":\"Benchmark named regional competitors by comparable price per 100 grams, animal-protein price gap, SKU format, and observed channel availability.\",\"must_mention\":[\"Growthwell\",\"Let's Plant Meat\",\"Green Rebel\"],\"reason\":\"Global-brand summaries miss local price points, culturally adapted products, and incumbent distribution advantages.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Growthwell Let's Plant Meat Green Rebel prices Singapore Thailand Indonesia retail\"]},{\"kind\":\"format\",\"requirement\":\"Present a phased 24-month country-channel sequence with quantified KPI gates, budget ranges, expansion triggers, and accountable executive owners.\",\"must_mention\":[],\"reason\":\"Execution readiness requires prioritization, measurable stop-go decisions, resource commitments, and ownership rather than an undifferentiated menu of channels.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Southeast Asia plant based food